															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sangosho" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1610303); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1610303&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200893" alt="Sangosho" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1610303)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sangosho" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1610303); return false;" >Sangosho</a></span> <span class="odate time_1576233640 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Dec 2019 10:40</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4445219">
									

<p>Hey! I'm a pretty infrequent Foundation user, but I'm a professional pianist. I'm commenting to suggest some edits to tone. A lot of my stuff is nitpicky, but I think it could help immersion if you implemented it, so I hope you will consider it.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4562 is a black Bösendorfer concert grand piano.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Can I suggest that you make it specifically a Bösendorfer Model 290 &quot;Imperial&quot;? This is because the article image depicts an Imperial, and because the Imperial has 9 more keys than standard, which seems relevant to your anomalous effects.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Low-note keys &#8230; High-note keys &#8230; Black keys</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't think I 100% understand how this can work. When I play almost anything, I usually have to use white keys and black keys across the range, without preference. It seems like the implication of this section is that a person would instantly be struck by a mess of conflicting memories.</p>
<p>You define a melody as</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Any coherent set of notes that together form a music piece.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How do you define 'coherent'? Many pieces of modern classical music would not be considered analytical in terms of common practice music theory.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A white envelope with the words &quot;My Music&quot; written on its front side</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How large is this envelope? I ask because you mentioned that Beethoven's 'Ode to Joy' is in it. The 'Ode' movement is about 24 minutes long; that's a lot of pages, in any format. Speaking of which -</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the other 22 were famous music pieces such as Beethoven's Ode to Joy.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>1) I would suggest not saying that it's the Ode, but rather 'a reduction of [the Ode] for solo piano'. This is because I think it's unlikely that anyone is playing from the unreduced orchestral score, but the ambiguity exists in the article as written.</p>
<p>2) I wouldn't reference it solely as the 'Ode to Joy'; technically, that's the poem. Even 'Beethoven's Ode to Joy' could mean &quot;another composer's setting of Beethoven's 1803 version of the text&quot;. I would recommend referencing it as 'the 4th movement of Ludwig van Beethoven's Symphony No. 9, Op. 125 (the &quot;Ode to Joy&quot;)'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Chopin's Funeral March</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would recommend updating this to 'Frederic Chopin's Piano Sonata No. 2 in B-flat minor, Op. 35: III. Marche funebre - Lento'</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

